{
  "$schema-note": "One document per row of the silent-loss table in docs/test-findings.md, with the note that row must produce. Read by src/features/registry/lossCorpus.test.ts, which runs every case and DERIVES the verdict rather than reading one. A case whose note fires is `lossy, told`; a case whose note does not fire is `lossy, silent`; a row with `input: null` is `not verified`. Most of this file is red on the day it lands, and that is the point: a ratio has a denominator, and a denominator is a thing somebody can add to.",
  "howToAddACase": "Append an object with the next `row` number, a unique `id`, the `tool` and `options` that reproduce it, an `input` that loses something, a `clean` input of the same shape that does not, the `expect` the note must satisfy, and the `drawn` block below. Nothing else has to change: the test derives the table and the ratio, the doc block it writes into docs/conversion-matrix.md is compared rather than maintained, and scripts/cross-browser-check.mjs drives the new row on /tools and on a canvas node in both engines on its next run. Two limits of that harness, both checked by lossCorpus.test.ts: it reads the tool's answer from a text box labelled '<Tool name> Converted', so a row is possible only for a tool whose first output is text, labelled Converted, with no view of its own; and drawn.choose can only pick from a select, so an option typed into a text field cannot be set on the page and has to be carried in the row's input instead.",
  "drawnNote": "`drawn` is what scripts/cross-browser-check.mjs holds each row to in two engines, beside `expect`: `choose` is the tool page's options as the page labels them (checked against the tool's own fields by lossCorpus.test.ts), `says` words the warning must carry as well as `expect.mentions`, `unsaid` words no note may carry, `face` what the node's face must hold (the subject when absent), `spoken` what its accessible name must, `outputLacks` what the converted text may not hold, `quiet` that the clean document draws no note at all, and `controls` more documents that lose nothing - each run through the same checks as `clean`, here and in the unit test.",
  "roundsThatReportedZero": [
